#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef942b09-8c13-51a3-8ad0-c6f691e4a472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287211} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7b50e6-5e79-547f-a4d0-3523fadcd174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287212} {:answer/id #uuid "73a99681-4e73-57fa-9774-b62220810b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287213} {:answer/id #uuid "6c60c08f-c067-5560-9388-dcc9cd508b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287214} {:answer/id #uuid "784b28ce-6ef1-5c7e-98aa-25f80beca79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef942b09-8c13-51a3-8ad0-c6f691e4a472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287211} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2bc329b-00e9-5c97-812b-73d35b80342e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2b9f164-4690-5b86-8b79-bf8259387b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-114133" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3,$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 1 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2(3-0) \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4f2682f-d8fe-540f-b2cc-bd5707f362bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287242} {:answer/id #uuid "e69df18e-b95f-585c-a50b-3ac706dbb4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2682f-d8fe-540f-b2cc-bd5707f362bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287242} :answer-field/id #uuid "06ece43e-61c9-59cc-98ed-5ab9945b8915" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d72efcd-4dfe-5261-8d75-86d82c839657" :question/math-academy-id "q-346051" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-7,$ calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(7)\\,-\\,x\\,{|}_{1}^{6} \\\\  & =14\\,-\\,(6-1) \\\\  & =14\\,-\\,5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa12a659-9565-53af-b941-8827250b633b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa12a659-9565-53af-b941-8827250b633b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287257} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ddec81-4b31-587c-8e65-9645ef73232a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18cab19d-8f67-5d6e-9e84-4c7aa4d64f7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346225" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=9,\\,\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=10.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the adjacent intervals rule and substituting known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the sum rule for definite integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(9)+(-8) \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =19-{\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
